(*
   Grammar of the Mialu programming language, in EBNF.

   The grammar works directly on the characters of a source file, without a separate tokenizer
   step. So it spells out where spaces may appear, and indentation is part of it. It describes
   what the compiler in src/ accepts today, which is only a part of the language; the language
   itself is described in docs/mialu-programming-language.md. Check a source file against it with
   scripts/verify_grammar.py.

   Notation, which is ISO 14977 EBNF with three small extensions:

     name = ... ;     defines a rule
     a , b            a followed by b
     a | b            a or b
     [ a ]            a, or nothing
     { a }            a repeated zero or more times
     ( a )            grouping
     a - b            a, except where b matches exactly the same text
     "text" 'text'    the characters in between
     ? ... ?          a special sequence, here "end of input" and "any character"
     comment          text between an opening parenthesis with an asterisk and an asterisk with a
                      closing parenthesis, like this one; comments do not nest

   Extensions to ISO 14977:
     - Terminals understand the escapes \n, \r, \t, \\, \" and \'.
     - "a" .. "z"     any one character from "a" to "z", both included.
     - Rule names are written in snake_case, without spaces.

   What a grammar cannot say, and the compiler checks in addition:
     - An integer literal must fit in a signed 64-bit integer.
     - A name that is used must be a variable or a parameter of its procedure or a constant or a
       global variable of the module, a variable can only be used below its definition, and no name
       may be declared twice, for example a variable may not have the name of a parameter. This is
       scope resolution, which the compiler does after parsing.
     - A program that has a foreign block must have exactly one `import foreign js` above the
       first of them, and no more. A foreign procedure takes and gives `Int`, `Bool`, `Str` or a
       struct, as any other procedure does, and an array only as a slice. The name of the object
       is not checked, because it is not known until the program runs.
     - The only attribute is `static`, it cannot be given twice, and every global variable must
       have it, because a global variable has static storage duration. A global variable can be
       used in every procedure of the module, also above where it is defined, and it can be assigned
       to like a variable. The type of a global variable must make sense for the target too, and
       the literal that it is given must be of that type.
     - The types in a program must make sense for the target, for example `Str` has no addition,
       which the grammar cannot tell from `Int`, and the value of a variable must be of the type
       that the variable is given.
     - A line may be indented by any number of spaces that is a multiple of 4. The grammar only
       has blocks nested up to the fourth level, see `block_1`. Deeper nesting is accepted by the
       compiler, up to 32 blocks inside each other, and the grammar needs more rules for it.
     - `length_of` is a built-in procedure, so it is a call of a name that nothing in the program
       defines. It takes one array or array slice.
     - The name of a call must be a procedure, or a variable, a parameter or a global variable that
       holds a pointer to a procedure, and the arguments must match the parameters in number and in
       type. A call of a procedure that returns nothing can only be a statement. The name of a
       procedure that is not called is a pointer to it, and it is accepted where a pointer to a
       procedure of the same parameter types and return type is, and so is `nil`. Calling `nil`
       traps.
     - The first number and the end of a range must be `Int`, and the iterator of a for statement is
       a variable of its block that cannot be assigned to, and that no other name may have. A name
       in the range cannot be the iterator of the same statement. All are checked after parsing.
     - A `break` must be inside a loop or a scope, and a `continue` must be inside the block of a
       for statement or a loop statement. The label of a `break` must be the label of a statement
       that the `break` is inside of, and the label of a `continue` the one of a loop that it is
       inside of. A statement cannot have the label of a statement around it. Labels are separate
       from the names of variables.
     - The condition of an if statement must be a `Bool`, so a number or a string is not one, and
       the operands of a comparison must both be `Int`, or both `Bool` for `==` and `not=`. A
       variable defined in a block can only be used in that block. All are checked after parsing.
     - The elements of an array and of an array slice must be `Int`, `Bool`, `Str` or a struct, an
       array literal must have as many elements as the length of its type when that is a number,
       and a variable of an array type must be given an array literal. What is subscripted, sliced or gone through by a for
       statement must be an array or an array slice, and an index must be an `Int`. An array can
       be a variable, but not a parameter, a global variable or a returned value. The grammar
       cannot tell a name that is an array from any other. A for statement with a range and one
       with elements both have an expression after `in`, and the range operator tells them apart.
     - The name of the type of a variable, a parameter or a field, and the name of a struct literal,
       must be `Int`, `Bool`, `Str` or a struct that is defined, also after the carets of a pointer
       type. A field of a struct cannot be an array, and a struct cannot contain itself, directly or
       through other structs, but it can have a pointer to its own struct. The fields of
       a struct have different names. In a struct literal every field must be a field of the
       struct, must be given a value at most once, and must be given a value of its type, and the
       literal `{ ... }` without a name is for a field that is a struct. A field is accessed
       through a variable, a parameter, an element of an array or what a pointer points to, and not
       through the result of a call. The grammar allows the lines of a literal between braces to be
       indented by any number of spaces, but the compiler requires a multiple of 4, as everywhere.
     - The type that an enum writes after `enum` is the type of the numbers of its members, and the
       only one there is is `Int`, so `enum Byte ->` is an error. The name of a type must be an enum
       too when it is the name of one. A member of an enum must
       be a member that the enum has. The enum of a member that is written with a dot only must be
       known from the place of the member, which is the type of the variable that it is the value
       of, of what it is assigned to, of the result that it is returned as, of the parameter, the
       field or the element that it is given to, or of what it is compared with. A member cannot be
       the value of a variable that is defined without a type. Members are compared with `==` and
       `not=` when they are of one enum, and have no order. A global variable is never an enum.
     - `nil` is a pointer of any type: it can be given to a variable, a field or an element of a
       pointer type, passed and returned where a pointer is wanted, and compared with `==` and
       `not=` with a pointer. It cannot be the value of a variable that is defined without a type,
       and `nil^` and `%nil` mean nothing. Two pointers are compared when they have the same type.
     - A field of a pointer to a struct is accessed as the field of the struct, `p.age`, or `p^.age`
       with the pointer followed explicitly. A field is not assigned through a parameter or the
       iterator of a for statement, but it is through a pointer, which only the types tell.
     - `p^` must follow a pointer, and the value that is assigned through it must be of the type
       that the pointer points to. `%` must be followed by something that has an address: a
       variable of a procedure, a field or an element of one, or what a pointer points to, and not a
       parameter, the iterator of a for statement, a global variable, a value such as a number or a
       call, an array or an array slice. There is no pointer to an array or to an array slice, so
       the caret of a pointer type is followed by a name or by another caret. A return type is
       never an array or an array slice either, and a global variable is never a pointer, because
       its value is a literal and no literal is a pointer. All of this the grammar cannot tell.
*)

module = { blank_line | definition | foreign_import | foreign_block } , end_of_input ;

(* A definition at file scope: a constant, or a global variable. *)
definition = constant_definition | global_variable_definition ;

(* `import foreign js "functions"` says that the procedures of the foreign blocks come from the
   JavaScript object that has the name between the quotes, which the program that runs the module
   passes to it when it instantiates it: the object `functions` in `{ functions: { ... } }`. `js`
   is the only foreign target there is. Like a definition it starts in the first column. *)
foreign_import =
    "import" , required_space , "foreign" , required_space , "js" , ws , string_literal , ws ,
    line_end ;

(* A foreign block lists procedures which are not in the program but in JavaScript: `foreign js ->`
   and below it, one level deeper, one line for each. Such a procedure has `...` where the body of
   a procedure would be, and it is called like any other. Its parameters and its return type are
   the ones that JavaScript receives and has to give back. Like a definition it starts in the
   first column, and the program may have any number of blocks. *)
foreign_block = "foreign" , required_space , "js" , branch_end , foreign_body ;

foreign_body = { blank_line } , foreign_procedure , { blank_line | foreign_procedure } ;

foreign_procedure =
    indentation_1 , name , ws , ":" , ws , ":" , ws ,
    "proc" , ws , "(" , ws , [ parameters , ws ] , ")" , ws , [ return_type , ws ] , "->" , ws ,
    "..." , ws , line_end ;

(* A constant definition. It starts in the first column, so a line that starts with a space or a
   tab does not match here. `::` is two separate ':' characters for the compiler, so spaces may
   be between them. The value is a procedure, a struct, an enum, the member of an enum such as
   `Color.RED`, or a string on one line. *)
constant_definition =
    name , ws , ":" , ws , ":" , ws ,
    ( procedure | string_literal , ws , line_end | struct_definition | enum_definition |
      enum_member_value ) ;

(* A struct is a record type: `struct ->` and then its fields, one on each line, indented one level
   deeper, each of them `name: Type`. The type of a field can be `struct ->` again, followed by
   the fields of that struct one level deeper still, so a struct can be inside a struct. Like the
   blocks, the rules go down to the fourth level. *)
struct_definition = "struct" , branch_end , struct_body_1 ;

(* An enum is a type with a fixed set of members: `enum Int ->` and then its members, one on each line,
   indented one level deeper, each of them a name. There is at least one member. The members are
   numbered from zero, in the order they are written. Their names only have to differ from each
   other, so two enums can have members of one name. A member is written with the name of its
   enum, such as `Color.RED`, or with a dot only, as in `.RED`, where the compiler knows the enum
   from the place that it is in. *)
enum_definition = "enum" , required_space , type_name , branch_end , enum_body ;

enum_body = { blank_line } , enum_member , { blank_line | enum_member } ;

enum_member = indentation_1 , name , ws , line_end ;

(* The value of a constant that is the member of an enum. *)
enum_member_value = name , ws , "." , ws , name , ws , line_end ;

struct_body_1 = { blank_line } , struct_field_1 , { blank_line | struct_field_1 } ;
struct_field_1 =
    indentation_1 , name , ws , ":" , ws ,
    ( type , ws , line_end | "struct" , branch_end , struct_body_2 ) ;

struct_body_2 = { blank_line } , struct_field_2 , { blank_line | struct_field_2 } ;
struct_field_2 =
    indentation_2 , name , ws , ":" , ws ,
    ( type , ws , line_end | "struct" , branch_end , struct_body_3 ) ;

struct_body_3 = { blank_line } , struct_field_3 , { blank_line | struct_field_3 } ;
struct_field_3 =
    indentation_3 , name , ws , ":" , ws ,
    ( type , ws , line_end | "struct" , branch_end , struct_body_4 ) ;

struct_body_4 = { blank_line } , struct_field_4 , { blank_line | struct_field_4 } ;
struct_field_4 = indentation_4 , name , ws , ":" , ws , type , ws , line_end ;

(* A global variable, which is also defined in the first column. It has static storage duration:
   it exists for as long as the program runs, and it is initialized once, before any procedure is
   called. Its value is a literal, or a field of the information about a type, which is known when
   the program is compiled, because nothing runs before it is set, and it keeps what the
   procedures assign to it from one call to the next. The attribute list comes right after the
   name, and `static` in it is required. Without a type the type of the variable is the type of the
   literal. `counter #(static) := 0` is an `Int`, `ready #(static) : Bool = false` gives the type. *)
global_variable_definition =
    name , ws , [ attribute_list , ws ] , ":" , ws , [ type , ws ] , "=" , ws ,
    global_value , ws , line_end ;

global_value =
    integer_literal | bool_literal | string_literal | type_info_of , { ws , postfix } ;

(* An attribute list starts with `#`, and has one or more attributes, each of them between
   parentheses: `#(static)`, or `#(first)(second)`. An attribute is a name. The compiler knows
   `static` only, and rejects any other. *)
attribute_list = "#" , ws , attribute , { ws , attribute } ;

attribute = "(" , ws , name , ws , ")" ;

(* A procedure and its body. The optional name before the arrow is the return type. *)
procedure =
    "proc" , ws , "(" , ws , [ parameters , ws ] , ")" , ws , [ return_type , ws ] , "->" , ws ,
    line_end , procedure_body ;

(* One or more parameters, separated by commas. There is no comma after the last one. *)
parameters = parameter , { ws , "," , ws , parameter } ;

(* The name of the parameter, and its type. *)
parameter = name , ws , ":" , ws , type ;

(* A type is a name, such as `Int`, a pointer type, or an array type. A pointer type is a caret and
   the type that is pointed to, which is a name or another pointer type: `^Int` is a pointer to an
   `Int`, and `^^Int` a pointer to a pointer to an `Int`. An array type is written with brackets
   before the type of the elements, which is a name or a pointer type: `[5]Int` is an array of five
   elements, `[const]Int` an array whose length is the number of elements of the array literal that
   it starts, which only makes sense there, `[]Int` an array slice, which views the elements of an
   array, and `[]^Int` an array slice of pointers.

   A pointer to a procedure is written with the carets and then `proc`, the types of the
   parameters between parentheses, and the return type, which is optional: `^proc(Int, Int) Int`.
   The caret is needed, because what a variable holds is the pointer and not the procedure, and a
   caret more makes a pointer to such a pointer: `^^proc(Int) Int`. The parameters of a procedure
   type have no names, and are not procedure types themselves, and the return type of a procedure
   or of a procedure type is never one either. *)
type = element_type | array_type ;

element_type = plain_type | procedure_pointer_type ;

(* A name, or the carets of a pointer type and then a name, and never a procedure type. *)
plain_type = type_name | "^" , ws , plain_type ;

type_name = name ;

procedure_pointer_type = "^" , ws , { "^" , ws } , procedure_type ;

procedure_type =
    "proc" , ws , "(" , ws , [ plain_type , { ws , "," , ws , plain_type } , ws ] , ")" ,
    [ ws , return_type ] ;

array_type = "[" , ws , [ array_length , ws ] , "]" , ws , element_type ;

array_length = integer_literal | "const" ;

return_type = plain_type ;

(* At least one statement. Blank lines are allowed before, between and after statements, and are
   not statements. The body is indented exactly one level, 4 spaces. A tab, or any other number of
   spaces, does not match. *)
procedure_body = block_1 ;

(* A grammar has no way to say "one level deeper than the line above", so there is a set of rules
   for each level of indentation. A block is the lines of a body, or of a branch of an if
   statement, which is indented one level deeper than the `if`. A block has at least one
   statement, and blank lines are allowed before, between and after them, so a blank line before an
   `else` belongs to the block above it. The rules go down to the fourth level, which is the body
   and three if, for, loop or scope statements inside each other. The compiler accepts 32 levels of
   blocks, and deeper nesting would be more sets of rules like these. *)
block_1 = { blank_line } , statement_1 , { blank_line | statement_1 } ;
statement_1 =
    simple_statement_1 | if_statement_1 | for_statement_1 | loop_statement_1 |
    scope_statement_1 ;
simple_statement_1 =
    indentation_1 ,
    ( return_statement | variable_definition | assignment | call | jump_statement ) , ws ,
    line_end ;
if_statement_1 =
    indentation_1 , [ label , required_space ] , "if" , required_space , condition ,
    branch_end , block_2 ,
    { indentation_1 , "else" , required_space , "if" , required_space , condition , branch_end ,
      block_2 } ,
    [ indentation_1 , "else" , branch_end , block_2 ] ;
for_statement_1 =
    indentation_1 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , required_space , name ,
    required_space , "in" , required_space ,
    iteration , branch_end , block_2 ;
loop_statement_1 =
    indentation_1 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , branch_end , block_2 ;
scope_statement_1 = indentation_1 , [ label ] , branch_end , block_2 ;

block_2 = { blank_line } , statement_2 , { blank_line | statement_2 } ;
statement_2 =
    simple_statement_2 | if_statement_2 | for_statement_2 | loop_statement_2 |
    scope_statement_2 ;
simple_statement_2 =
    indentation_2 ,
    ( return_statement | variable_definition | assignment | call | jump_statement ) , ws ,
    line_end ;
if_statement_2 =
    indentation_2 , [ label , required_space ] , "if" , required_space , condition ,
    branch_end , block_3 ,
    { indentation_2 , "else" , required_space , "if" , required_space , condition , branch_end ,
      block_3 } ,
    [ indentation_2 , "else" , branch_end , block_3 ] ;
for_statement_2 =
    indentation_2 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , required_space , name ,
    required_space , "in" , required_space ,
    iteration , branch_end , block_3 ;
loop_statement_2 =
    indentation_2 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , branch_end , block_3 ;
scope_statement_2 = indentation_2 , [ label ] , branch_end , block_3 ;

block_3 = { blank_line } , statement_3 , { blank_line | statement_3 } ;
statement_3 =
    simple_statement_3 | if_statement_3 | for_statement_3 | loop_statement_3 |
    scope_statement_3 ;
simple_statement_3 =
    indentation_3 ,
    ( return_statement | variable_definition | assignment | call | jump_statement ) , ws ,
    line_end ;
if_statement_3 =
    indentation_3 , [ label , required_space ] , "if" , required_space , condition ,
    branch_end , block_4 ,
    { indentation_3 , "else" , required_space , "if" , required_space , condition , branch_end ,
      block_4 } ,
    [ indentation_3 , "else" , branch_end , block_4 ] ;
for_statement_3 =
    indentation_3 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , required_space , name ,
    required_space , "in" , required_space ,
    iteration , branch_end , block_4 ;
loop_statement_3 =
    indentation_3 , [ label , required_space ] , "for" , branch_end , block_4 ;
scope_statement_3 = indentation_3 , [ label ] , branch_end , block_4 ;

block_4 = { blank_line } , statement_4 , { blank_line | statement_4 } ;
statement_4 = simple_statement_4 ;
simple_statement_4 =
    indentation_4 ,
    ( return_statement | variable_definition | assignment | call | jump_statement ) , ws ,
    line_end ;

(* A loop statement runs its block again and again, until a `break` or a `return` leaves it. It is a
   `for` without an iterator and a range. *)

(* A scope statement is a block that runs once, in a scope of its own: a `->` alone on a line, with
   the block indented one level deeper. A `break` inside it leaves it. It can have a label, which
   is written `@` and a name with no space in between, such as `@outer ->`. An if statement, a
   for statement and a loop statement can have a label before the `if` or the `for`, such as
   `@outer if x > 1 ->` or `@outer for ->`, which is separated from it by a space. A `break` or a
   `continue` names the label with the name only, without the `@`. *)
label = "@" , name ;

(* A for statement runs its block once for each number of a range, or once for each element of an
   array or an array slice. The name is the iterator, which is an `Int` that exists only in the
   block. The range has a first number and an end, and `..<` leaves the end out of the range, while
   `..<=` keeps it. Both are expressions that the compiler requires to be `Int`, and they cannot use
   the iterator. What is not a range is an expression that the compiler requires to be an array or
   an array slice, as in `for x in elements ->`. The `->` ends the line, and what is indented below
   it is the block. *)
iteration = range | expression ;

range = expression , ws , ( "..<=" | "..<" ) , ws , expression ;

(* `break` leaves the innermost loop or scope, or the statement that has the label after it, and
   `continue` goes on with the next pass of the innermost loop, or of the loop that has the label
   after it. The compiler requires a `break` to be inside a loop or a scope and the label to be one
   of a statement around it, and a `continue` to be inside the block of a for statement or a loop
   statement, which may have if statements and scopes in between, and its label to be the one of a
   loop. *)
jump_statement = ( "break" | "continue" ) , [ required_space , name ] ;

(* The `if` with its first condition, any number of `else if`, and at most one `else` that comes
   last. The `->` ends the line, and what is indented below it is the block of the branch. *)
branch_end = ws , "->" , ws , line_end ;

(* The condition of a branch is an expression. The compiler requires it to be a `Bool`, which the
   grammar cannot tell, because a name or a comparison can be one. *)
condition = expression ;

comparison_operator = "==" | "not=" | "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ;

indentation_1 = "    " ;
indentation_2 = "        " ;
indentation_3 = "            " ;
indentation_4 = "                " ;

(* A variable and its initial value. `:=` is a ':' and a '=' for the compiler, so spaces may be
   between them, as for `::`. Without a type the type of the variable is the type of the value.
   `x : Int = 5` gives the type. *)
variable_definition =
    name , ws , ":" , ws , [ type , ws ] , "=" , ws , expression |
    name , ws , ":" , ws , type ;

(* A variable can leave its value out when its type is written, as in `p : Person`. It then has the
   default value of its type: zero for an `Int`, `false` for a `Bool`, the empty string for a
   `Str`, an empty array slice for an array slice, and the default of each field for a struct. *)

(* An assignment to a variable that is already defined: `x += 2` adds 2 to `x`, `x -= 2`
   subtracts 2 from it, `x *= 2` multiplies it by 2, and `x /= 2` divides it by 2. The operator
   is a single token, so no space may be written inside it. The name must be a variable, not a
   parameter or a constant, which the grammar cannot tell. The same operators change an element of
   an array: `a[1] += 2`, and a field of a struct: `p.age += 1`. A plain `=` gives a variable a
   new value, as in `x = 2` or `p = Person { }`, assigns to an element or a field, as in
   `a[1] = 2` and `p.age = 2`, or copies to a range of elements, as in `a[0:3] = b`. The same
   operators and `=` change what a pointer points to: `p^ += 1`, `p^ = 2` and `p.age = 2`. *)
assignment = name , { ws , postfix } , ws , ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "=" ) , ws , expression ;

(* `return` and its value must be separated, or `return0` would be one identifier. *)
return_statement = "return" , required_space , expression ;

(* The operators from the loosest to the tightest are `or`, `and`, `not`, a comparison, `+` and
   `-`, and `*` and `/`. `or` and `and` are left associative, so `a or b or c` is
   `(a or b) or c`, and `not` may be repeated. `and`, `or` and `not` take `Bool` values, which the
   grammar cannot tell. They are words, so a space must separate them from what is next to them,
   unlike the symbols. There are no parentheses yet, so the precedence is the only way to group. *)
expression = disjunction ;

disjunction = conjunction , { required_space , "or" , required_space , conjunction } ;

conjunction = negation , { required_space , "and" , required_space , negation } ;

negation = { "not" , required_space } , comparison ;

(* A sum, or a comparison of two sums, which does not chain: `a < b` is a comparison, `a < b < c`
   is not. A comparison has the type `Bool`. *)
comparison = sum , [ ws , comparison_operator , ws , sum ] ;

(* Terms joined by `+` or `-`, which have the same precedence. The compiler reads `a - b + c` as
   `(a - b) + c`, which a grammar that only accepts or rejects text does not show. Spaces around
   the operator are optional. *)
sum = term , { ws , ( "+" | "-" ) , ws , term } ;

(* Operands joined by `*` or `/`, which have the same precedence and bind tighter than `+` and `-`:
   the compiler reads `a + b * c` as `a + (b * c)`, which the grammar does not show either. *)
term = operand , { ws , ( "*" | "/" ) , ws , operand } ;

(* An operand, with any number of `%` before it, each of which takes the address of what follows. It
   binds tighter than every operator, but looser than the postfixes after a name, so `%p.x` is the
   address of the field `p.x`, and `%a[2]` the address of the element `a[2]`. The compiler requires
   the operand of `%` to have an address, which the grammar cannot tell. *)
operand = { "%" , ws } , operand_value ;

(* An integer literal, a string literal, `true` or `false`, a call of a procedure, or the use of a
   name, such as a parameter or a constant, or an array literal or a struct literal. A call starts
   with a name too, and is told from the use of a name by the opening parenthesis after it, and a
   struct literal by the brace. A name and the literals can have subscripts, fields and carets
   after them. *)
operand_value =
    integer_literal | string_literal | bool_literal | nil_literal | enum_literal | call |
    ( name | array_literal | struct_literal | type_info_of ) , { ws , postfix } ;

(* The information about a type, which the compiler works out while it compiles the program:
   `type_info_of(Color)`. Only a field can follow it, such as `type_info_of(Color).members`, and
   the type must be an enum. The compiler checks both after parsing. *)
type_info_of = "type_info_of" , ws , "(" , ws , name , ws , ")" ;

(* The member of an enum that is written without the enum, such as `.RED`. *)
enum_literal = "." , name ;

(* What can follow a name or a literal: a subscript, a field, such as `p.age`, or a caret, which
   follows a pointer to give what it points to, as in `p^`. They can follow each other, as in
   `lines[1].end.y` and `node.next.value`. *)
postfix = subscript | field_access | dereference ;

field_access = "." , ws , name ;

dereference = "^" ;

(* A subscript is the index of an element between brackets, `a[2]`, or the two indices of an array
   slice, `a[1:4]`, which has the elements from the first index up to, and not including, the
   second. All of the indices are expressions that the compiler requires to be `Int`. *)
subscript = "[" , ws , expression , ws , [ ":" , ws , expression , ws ] , "]" ;

(* An array literal is the type of an array, which has a length or `const`, followed by its
   elements between braces. The elements are expressions separated by commas, and there may be none.
   They can be on lines of their own, and a comma can come after the last one. *)
array_literal =
    "[" , ws , array_length , ws , "]" , ws , element_type , ws , "{" , gap , [ elements , gap ] , "}" ;

elements = expression , { gap , "," , gap , expression } , [ gap , "," ] ;

(* A struct literal is the name of a struct and the fields that are given a value, between braces,
   in any order, as in `Person { name = "Bob", age = 30 }`. The fields that are left out get their
   default value. The fields are separated by commas, and can be on lines of their own, and a
   comma can come after the last one. A field that is a struct can be given its value without a
   name, `{ x = 1, y = 2 }`, since the field says which struct it is. *)
struct_literal = name , ws , struct_fields ;

struct_fields = "{" , gap , [ field_initializers , gap ] , "}" ;

field_initializers = field_initializer , { gap , "," , gap , field_initializer } , [ gap , "," ] ;

field_initializer = name , ws , "=" , ws , ( struct_fields | expression ) ;

(* Spaces and line breaks between the parts of a literal between braces. *)
gap = { " " | "\t" | "\r" | "\n" } ;

(* The name of a procedure, and the values to give to its parameters between parentheses, which
   are separated by commas and can be none. A call is an expression, and a line that is only a call
   is a statement, whose value is thrown away. The compiler checks that the name is a procedure,
   that there are as many arguments as the procedure has parameters, and that each argument has
   the type of its parameter. *)
call = name , ws , "(" , ws , [ arguments , ws ] , ")" ;

arguments = expression , { ws , "," , ws , expression } ;

bool_literal = "true" | "false" ;

(* `nil` is the null pointer. *)
nil_literal = "nil" ;

(* Text between quotes, on one line. It is taken as it is: there are no escape sequences yet, so a
   backslash is not allowed, to keep it free for them. Any other character is, including a tab
   and characters that are not ASCII. The grammar works on characters, so it only sees text that
   is valid UTF-8; the compiler works on bytes and rejects a string that is not. *)
string_literal = '"' , { string_character } , '"' ;

string_character = any_character - ( '"' | "\\" | "\n" | "\r" ) ;

any_character = ? any character ? ;

(* Names. An identifier that is a reserved word is not a name. A reserved word is a keyword or a
   literal word. *)
name = identifier - reserved_word ;

reserved_word = keyword | literal_word ;

keyword =
    "proc" | "return" | "if" | "else" | "for" | "in" | "break" | "const" | "continue" | "struct" |
    "enum" | "not" | "and" | "or" | "foreign" | "import" | "type_info_of" ;

(* The words that are literals: the two values of a `Bool` and the null pointer. *)
literal_word = "true" | "false" | "nil" ;

identifier = ( letter | "_" ) , { letter | digit | "_" } ;

(* A number is written in decimal, and a letter or underscore may not follow it directly. Both
   are guaranteed because only a space, an operator, a line end or the end of input is allowed after
   it. *)
integer_literal = digit , { digit } ;

letter = "a" .. "z" | "A" .. "Z" ;

digit = "0" .. "9" ;

(* Spaces inside a line. A carriage return counts as one, so lines may end with \r\n. *)
ws = { " " | "\t" | "\r" } ;

required_space = ( " " | "\t" | "\r" ) , ws ;

(* A line with nothing on it but spaces. It carries no indentation, so its width does not matter. *)
blank_line = ws , line_end ;

(* The last line of a file does not need a line feed. *)
line_end = "\n" | end_of_input ;

end_of_input = ? end of input ? ;
